samples.evidence:
mermaid.ts:199-211 states the two regexes are 'copied verbatim' from mermaid's `src/utils/regexes.ts`, and the test at preview.test.ts (`still targets the mermaid release its carrier patterns came from`) enforces that by comparing `MERMAID_CARRIER_PATTERNS_VERSION` against `require('mermaid/package.json').version`. Two gaps:

(1) The guard is a version equality check, not a pattern equality check. It proves nobody bumped the dependency; it proves nothing about whether the copies match upstream at the pinned version. If the initial transcription was wrong, the test is green forever.

(2) `FRONTMATTER_RE` at mermaid.ts:211 is `/^([^\S
]*)-{3}\s*[
](.*?)[
]\1-{3}\s*[
]+/s`, which carries an indentation capture group and a `\1` back-reference on the closing delimiter. To my recollection mermaid's `frontMatterRegex` has no such group (`/^-{3}\s*[
](.*?)[
]-{3}\s*[
]+/s`). The deviation happens to recognize a superset (safe direction), but it contradicts the 'verbatim' claim the whole guard rests on, and the `\1` back-reference means opening and closing indentation must agree — a divergence from the engine that a future edit could easily flip into the unsafe direction with no test to catch it.

Searched: `Grep directiveRegex|frontMatterRegex` across the repo (hits only mermaid.ts, preview.test.ts, docs/design/notes/notes.md and .agent-review artifacts — no vendored upstream copy to diff against); `Glob **/node_modules/mermaid/package.json` and `**/node_modules/mermaid/dist/chunks/mermaid.core/chunk-*.mjs` (no matches — mermaid is not installed in this working tree, so I could not diff the installed patterns). A stronger guard would assert on the regex `source` strings extracted from the installed engine's bundle, or fail closed when a `%%{`/leading `---` survives the strip.
